\section{Core Material Subsystems}

\subsection{Subsystem Alpha: Non-Linear Elastic--Plastic Strain}

This subsystem evaluates micro-fracture propagation, compressive yielding, and 
shear failure under intense relativistic gravitational gradients. The governing 
yield criterion is the extended Drucker--Prager plasticity model:

\begin{equation}
F = \sqrt{J_2} + \alpha p - k = 0,
\end{equation}

where $J_2$ is the second invariant of the deviatoric stress tensor, 
$p$ is the hydrostatic pressure, $\alpha$ is the frictional parameter, 
and $k$ is the cohesion hardening variable. This formulation enables UV-SIM 
to track pressure-dependent yielding, shear band formation, and non-linear 
plastic flow across heterogeneous material layers.

\subsection{Subsystem Beta: Acoustic Impedance and Shock Propagation}

This subsystem simulates high-frequency shock wave attenuation through dense 
aggregate boundaries. The heterogeneous wave equation governing particle 
displacement $u_i$ is:

\begin{equation}
\rho \frac{\partial^2 u_i}{\partial t^2}
=
\frac{\partial}{\partial x_j}
\left(
C_{ijkl}
\frac{\partial u_k}{\partial x_l}
\right),
\end{equation}

where $\rho$ is the material density and $C_{ijkl}$ is the elasticity tensor. 
This model captures impedance mismatch, shock reflection, attenuation profiles, 
and non-linear acoustic damping across multi-phase concrete analogues.

